'''Emigration from Balisca''' is the process where citizens from the [[Baliscan Confederation]] move to live in countries other than Balisca, creating a '''Baliscan Diaspora''' ('''Overseas Baliscans'''). The process is the reverse of the [[immigration to Balisca]]. Emigration from Balisca [[Baliscan post-revolution exodus|peaked during the early 1920s]] a result of the political and economic instability and violence during the [[Baliscan Revolution]], predominately consisting of more conservative elites and their families.

The diaspora is concentrated in places that were part of the [[W:Spanish Empire|Spanish Empire]]. Countries with sizable Baliscan populations are [[Argentina]], [[W:Bolivia|Bolivia]], [[Brazil]], [[W:Chile|Chile]], [[W:Colombia|Colombia]], [[W:Costa Rica|Costa Rica]], [[W:Cuba|Cuba]], [[W:Dominican Republic|Dominican Republic]], [[W:Ecuador|Ecuador]], [[W:El Salvador|El Salvador]], [[W:Guatemala|Guatemala]], [[W:Honduras|Honduras]], [[W:Nicaragua|Nicaragua]], [[W:Panama|Panama]], [[W:Paraguay|Paraguay]], [[W:Peru|Peru]], [[W:Uruguay|Uruguay]], [[W:Venezuela|Venezuela]], and, to a lesser extent, [[Mexico]], [[Formosa]], [[Haiti]], the [[United States]], [[California]], the [[Philippines]] and [[W:Europe|Europe]].

According to the latest data from the [[Confederal Institute of Statistics]]' Register of Baliscans Resident Abroad (''Registro de Baliscanas Residentes en el Extranjero''), "the number of people with Baliscan nationality living abroad was 7,097,530 on 1 January 2024, a decrease of 1.8% (127,755 people) with respect to the data from the same day last year". 

